# tests/startset/dfahat_paths.rxt -- [START-SET] stage 3, THE DFA HAT's
# LANDING-PATH witnesses (docs/design/startset.md §6.4.3 item 3). Hand-written
# and python-verified (tests/harness/verify_rxt.py; libpcre2 agrees).
#
# The bounded skip lands two ways: on a byte of T (the HIT path) or, with no
# such byte before n-1, at n-1 (the CLAMP path). Each landing re-seeds the
# state from the byte before it. Under a PERMISSIVE context (`\b` alone) a
# stale start state can only add a candidate the stepped loop rejects; under
# a RESTRICTIVE one (`\B`, a negative lookbehind) it forbids a real start.
# `\B(?<!a)d` (the one-byte `memchr` form) has the restrictive context and
# its witnesses live in dfahat.rxt/reseed.rxt; this block is the SET form's
# twin, the class `[de]`, so the clamp path of `first-class-bounded` has an
# answer witness of its own (sabotage S483).

# CLAMP path: the only T byte is the subject's LAST byte.
pattern \B(?<!a)[de]
features all
ms 0 "xd" 1 2
ms 1 "xd" 1 2
ns 2 "xd"
ms 0 "xxe" 2 3
ns 0 "ad"
ns 0 " d"
# HIT path: a T byte before n-1.
ms 0 "xdz" 1 2
ms 0 "yez" 1 2
ns 0 "adz"

# F's NON-NULLABLE conjunct (sabotage S488). The erased language of this
# pattern is nullable (the `\b\B` branch erases to empty), so the start set
# declines it, while the machine itself cannot match empty (`\b\B` is never
# true) and carries a seeded byte-class skip. Removing the conjunct admits it
# as a mover: answer-equivalent here (a machine whose start state cannot
# accept in any context has no empty match for a skip to pass), so this
# block's job is to be in the corpus, where tests/startset/dfahat_checks.py
# [dfa-movers] sees a mover the manifest's F does not admit.
pattern \b\B|\b(?:ab|cd)
features all
ms 0 "ab" 0 2
ms 0 "xab cd" 4 6
ms 1 " ab" 1 3
ns 0 "xab"
ns 0 ""
